[38;5;231m⏺[39m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m now accepts either a login session or a service token, and I tested it against a running server:

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┬────────┐
  │                 Request                 │ Result │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┤
  │ No credentials                          │ 401    │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <correct token>[39m   │ 200    │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┤
  │ Wrong token                             │ 401    │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┤
  │ Session cookie (login, as before)       │ 200    │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┤
  │ Env var unset, sending [38;5;153mBearer undefined[39m │ 401    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────┴────────┘

  [1mChanges:[0m
  - [1m[38;5;153mauth.js[39m:[0m added [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrServiceToken[39m. It checks the bearer token using a comparison that doesn't leak timing
    information, and falls back to the normal login check. If [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m isn't set, token access is off.
  - [1m[38;5;153mserver.js[39m:[0m the admin route now uses the new check.

  [1mWhat the dashboard team needs to do:[0m
  1. Set [38;5;153mREPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN[39m to a random value wherever the service runs, e.g. [38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m.
  2. Give the token to the dashboard team through a secret store, not chat.
  3. Their script sends it like this: [38;5;153mcurl -H "Authorization: Bearer $REPORTS_SERVICE_TOKEN" [39m
     [38;5;153mhttps://<host>/admin/reports[39m

  To test, I ran [38;5;153mnpm install[39m and then deleted the [38;5;153mnode_modules/[39m and [38;5;153mpackage-lock.json[39m it created, so only [38;5;153mauth.js[39m and
  [38;5;153mserver.js[39m have changed. Nothing is committed yet. Want me to commit it?

